.TH NVME_URING_CMD_MONITOR 1 "September 2026" "ebpf-syscall" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nvme_uring_cmd_monitor \- trace io_uring NVMe passthrough commands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nvme_uring_cmd_monitor
.RB [ \-\-pid " PID" ]
.RB [ \-\-dur " SECONDS" ]
.RB [ \-\-jsonl " PATH" ]
.RB [ \-\-lba-size " BYTES" ]
.RB [ \-\-kv ]
.RB [ \-\-quiesced ]
.RB [ \-\-no-cq-overflow ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nvme_uring_cmd_monitor
observes NVMe uring-command submissions and completions.  Normal namespace
mode decodes logical block addresses and lengths.  NVMe Key-Value mode decodes
store, retrieve, delete, and exist commands.  Output includes request identity,
timestamps, completion status and latency, and a final loss-accounting record.
.SH OBSERVATION POINT
The submission probes run at
.B nvme_ns_chr_uring_cmd
and
.BR nvme_ns_head_chr_uring_cmd ,
on each attempt to issue an NVMe passthrough SQE.  The completion probe runs at
.BR nvme_uring_cmd_end_io .
This is below the system-call batching boundary: one
.BR io_uring_enter (2)
can submit 64 SQEs.  Without a handler retry, a loss-free capture records 64
submissions and 64 completions.  SQPOLL can consume SQEs without a system call
per command.
This tree's
.BR syscall_monitor (1)
does not hook
.BR io_uring_enter (2)
at all.
.PP
Every io_uring SQE has a 64-bit
.B user_data
field.  The monitor can associate that cookie with an NVMe command because it
reads the embedded SQE on this passthrough path.  The kernel returns the cookie
in the CQE; it is not part of the command sent to the device.
.PP
At submission, the BPF program stores the issue time and cookie in a hash keyed
by
.BR "struct io_uring_cmd *" .
The completion hook looks up and deletes that entry, computes latency in the
kernel, and emits the completion through the ring buffer.  Require the final
loss and completeness fields to pass before calling the stream complete.
.PP
This is a Linux-driver witness, not a PCIe bus analyzer.  It does not cover
ordinary POSIX or file io_uring IO, controller firmware, FTL or NAND work, or
SPDK/VFIO paths that bypass the Linux NVMe driver.  Use
.BR nvme_tp_monitor (1)
for all requests that reach a selected Linux NVMe namespace.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-pid " PID"
Restrict process-scoped producers to one thread-group ID.
.TP
.BI \-\-dur " seconds"
Stop after the given duration.
.TP
.BI \-\-jsonl " path"
Write newline-delimited JSON to the path.
.TP
.BI \-\-lba-size " bytes"
Set the namespace logical block size used to decode normal block commands.
.TP
.B \-\-kv
Decode NVMe Key-Value commands instead of normal LBA commands.  This records
the 16-byte object key in the
.B key_hex
field.
.TP
.B \-\-quiesced
Assert that PID-scoped producers are stopped before the monitor is signaled,
allowing completion and loss accounting to drain under that operating rule.
.TP
.B \-\-no-cq-overflow
Disable system-wide completion-queue overflow probes.
.SH PRIVACY
This tool does not copy value payloads, but
.B \-\-kv
does copy object keys.  Process name, PID, TID, user_data, queue, command ID,
offsets or keys, sizes, and timing can all be sensitive.  Do not call a raw KV
capture anonymous and do not publish one without a field-by-field review.
.SH FIDELITY
Check the final loss and completeness fields before treating a capture as
complete.  This monitor covers the io_uring NVMe passthrough path; use
.BR nvme_tp_monitor (1)
for namespace-wide driver tracepoints independent of the submitting API.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kvio (1),
.BR syscall_monitor (1),
.BR iouring_monitor (1),
.BR nvme_tp_monitor (1)
